1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,675
The problem is called decoherence, and it is not a small engineering detail.

2
00:00:04,675 --> 00:00:07,275
It is the central fact of quantum computing.

3
00:00:07,275 --> 00:00:10,237
A qubit is a quantum system with two states.

4
00:00:10,237 --> 00:00:12,700
Not a bit that is both zero and one.

5
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:13,925
That is a cartoon.

6
00:00:13,925 --> 00:00:22,225
A qubit is a system described by two complex numbers, amplitudes, whose squares give the probability of measuring zero or one.

7
00:00:22,225 --> 00:00:25,162
Before measurement, it can be in a superposition.

8
00:00:25,162 --> 00:00:27,037
After measurement, it is not.

9
00:00:27,037 --> 00:00:29,387
Everything in between is coherence.

10
00:00:29,387 --> 00:00:35,887
The amplitudes have to stay in a precise relationship, with a precise phase, for the computation to work.

11
00:00:35,887 --> 00:00:38,862
And almost everything destroys that relationship.

12
00:00:38,862 --> 00:00:40,162
A stray photon.

13
00:00:40,162 --> 00:00:41,487
A vibrating atom.

14
00:00:41,487 --> 00:00:42,675
A cosmic ray.

15
00:00:42,675 --> 00:00:43,350
Heat.

16
00:00:43,350 --> 00:00:45,262
So the first thing you need is cold.

17
00:00:45,262 --> 00:00:46,787
Not refrigerator cold.

18
00:00:46,787 --> 00:00:48,225
Ten millikelvin.

19
00:00:48,225 --> 00:00:52,900
Colder than the cosmic microwave background, which is two point seven kelvin.

20
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:55,287
Colder than anything in the solar system.

21
00:00:55,287 --> 00:00:57,712
That is what a dilution refrigerator is for.

22
00:00:57,712 --> 00:01:07,425
It is the machine you have seen in photographs without knowing what you were looking at: a gold-plated chandelier, hanging in a cylinder, surrounded by layers of shielding.

23
00:01:07,425 --> 00:01:14,562
At the bottom, a chip the size of a fingernail, sitting at a temperature where the thermal noise is slower than the computation.

24
00:01:14,562 --> 00:01:16,875
The second thing you need is isolation.

25
00:01:16,875 --> 00:01:21,887
The chip is shielded from magnetic fields, from radio waves, from vibration.

26
00:01:21,887 --> 00:01:24,450
The room around it is a Faraday cage.

27
00:01:24,450 --> 00:01:33,812
The wiring that carries signals in is filtered, attenuated, and carefully routed, because a single stray microwave photon can ruin a calculation.

28
00:01:33,812 --> 00:01:35,825
And the third thing you need is speed.

29
00:01:35,825 --> 00:01:40,950
A superconducting qubit holds its state for tens or hundreds of microseconds.

30
00:01:40,950 --> 00:01:43,425
A gate takes tens of nanoseconds.

31
00:01:43,425 --> 00:01:45,375
That is a ratio of thousands.

32
00:01:45,375 --> 00:01:49,300
You have time for a few thousand operations before the state decays.

33
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:50,475
Not millions.

34
00:01:50,475 --> 00:01:51,412
Thousands.

35
00:01:51,412 --> 00:01:54,087
This is why error correction is not optional.

36
00:01:54,087 --> 00:01:57,475
The first real qubits were built in the late nineteen nineties.

37
00:01:57,475 --> 00:02:06,137
In nineteen ninety-five, Juan Ignacio Cirac and Peter Zoller proposed a way to trap ions with electric fields and manipulate them with lasers.

38
00:02:06,137 --> 00:02:13,687
In nineteen ninety-nine, a team at NEC in Japan, led by Yasunobu Nakamura, built a superconducting qubit.

39
00:02:13,687 --> 00:02:18,400
A tiny circuit with a Josephson junction, behaving like an artificial atom.

40
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:20,262
Both approaches are still with us.

41
00:02:20,262 --> 00:02:26,550
In two thousand, David DiVincenzo wrote down the five criteria a quantum computer has to satisfy.

42
00:02:26,550 --> 00:02:28,550
Qubits that can be initialized.

43
00:02:28,550 --> 00:02:30,425
A universal set of gates.

44
00:02:30,425 --> 00:02:32,012
Long coherence times.

45
00:02:32,012 --> 00:02:33,137
A way to read out.

46
00:02:33,137 --> 00:02:34,312
And a way to scale.

47
00:02:34,312 --> 00:02:36,837
That list is still the field's report card.

48
00:02:36,837 --> 00:02:40,187
For the next fifteen years, the numbers improved slowly.

49
00:02:40,187 --> 00:02:43,887
One qubit became two, then five, then twenty.

50
00:02:43,887 --> 00:02:48,887
Fidelities climbed from ninety percent to ninety-nine, then ninety-nine point nine.

51
00:02:48,887 --> 00:02:52,925
Each decimal place was a thesis, a career, a decade.

52
00:02:52,925 --> 00:03:01,362
And then, in twenty nineteen, Google put a machine called Sycamore in front of the world and claimed it had done something no classical computer could do.

53
00:03:01,362 --> 00:03:04,687
Sycamore had fifty-three superconducting qubits.

54
00:03:04,687 --> 00:03:10,575
The task was random circuit sampling: run a random sequence of gates, measure, repeat.

55
00:03:10,575 --> 00:03:12,350
It is deliberately useless.

56
00:03:12,350 --> 00:03:14,850
It produces no answer anyone wants.

57
00:03:14,850 --> 00:03:16,300
It is a stress test.

58
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:23,137
A way of asking whether the machine can hold a quantum state long enough to do something a classical computer cannot follow.

59
00:03:23,137 --> 00:03:29,412
Google said Sycamore had done in two hundred seconds what would take a classical supercomputer ten thousand years.

60
00:03:29,412 --> 00:03:31,612
IBM immediately disagreed.

61
00:03:31,612 --> 00:03:37,987
Their analysis said the same calculation could be done on a classical machine in two and a half days.

62
00:03:37,987 --> 00:03:39,575
Not ten thousand years.

63
00:03:39,575 --> 00:03:40,962
Two and a half days.

64
00:03:40,962 --> 00:03:42,725
Both numbers were defensible.

65
00:03:42,725 --> 00:03:45,012
That is the problem with the word supremacy.

66
00:03:45,012 --> 00:03:47,900
The claim was not that quantum computers are useful.

67
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:50,400
It was that a quantum machine had crossed a line.

68
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:57,525
And the line kept moving, because classical algorithms kept getting better, and classical hardware kept getting faster.

69
00:03:57,626 --> 00:04:01,126
What survived the argument was something more interesting than the claim.

70
00:04:01,126 --> 00:04:08,201
Sycamore proved that a programmable quantum machine with more than fifty qubits could be built, calibrated, and run.

71
00:04:08,201 --> 00:04:10,501
It proved that the engineering was real.

72
00:04:10,501 --> 00:04:13,326
The theory had been waiting thirty-five years for that.

